The observed counts are used to calculate the antimatter yield with appropriate normalization (the differential invariant yield) in order to compare with the theoretical expectation. The calculated ratios are \({}^{4}He\)/\({}^{3}He\)\(=(3.0\pm 1.3({\rm stat})^{+0.5}_{-0.3}({\rm sys}))\times 10^{-3}\) and \({}^{4}\overline{He}\)/\({}^{3}\overline{He}\)\(=(3.2\pm 2.3({\rm stat})^{+0.7}_{-0.2}({\rm sys}))\times 10^{-3}\) for central Au+Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}\) = 200 GeV. The differential yields (\(d^{2}N/(2\pi p_{T}dp_{T}dy)\)) for \({}^{4}He\)(\({}^{4}\overline{He}\)) are obtained by multiplying the ratio of \({}^{4}He\)/\({}^{3}He\) (\({}^{4}\overline{He}\)/\({}^{3}\overline{He}\)) with the \({}^{3}He\) (\({}^{3}\overline{He}\)) yields [12]. Figure 5.6 shows the exponential [13] invariant yields versus baryon number in 200 GeV central Au+Au collisions. Empirically, the production rate reduces by a factor of \(1.6^{+1.0}_{-0.6}\times 10^{3}\) (\(1.1^{+0.3}_{-0.2}\times 10^{3}\)) for each additional antinucleon (nucleon) added to the antinucleus (nucleus). This general trend is expected from coalescent nucleosynthesis models [14], originally developed to describe production of antideuterons [15], as well as from thermodynamic models [16].

