Summary

We have shown that \({}^{4}\overline{He}\) exists, and have measured its rate of production in nuclear interactions. This provides a benchmark for possible future observations of \({}^{4}\overline{He}\) in cosmic radiation. Barring a new breakthrough in accelerator technology, or the discovery of a completely new production mechanism, it is likely that the \({}^{4}\overline{He}\) will remain the heaviest stable antimatter nucleus observed in the foreseeable future.

